CAT = "ukcareRole"; SEG = "ROL"
# Standard 1 Understand your role, Standard 2 Your personal development
Q = [
("Where would you first look to check exactly which tasks are part of your job?",
 "Your job description", ["The weekly staff rota", "A colleague's handover notes", "The home's visitors' book"],
 "Your job description sets out your main duties and who you report to. Agreed ways of working and policies then describe how each task is done."),
("What does the phrase \"agreed ways of working\" mean in a care setting?",
 "Your employer's policies and procedures", ["Habits shared by the whole team", "Rules each worker writes for themselves", "Tips from the people you support"],
 "Agreed ways of working are the policies, procedures and guidance your employer has set out. They may be less formal in a small setting but you must still follow them."),
("Which body registers and inspects adult social care services in England?",
 "The Care Quality Commission", ["Skills for Care England", "The Health and Safety Executive", "The Local Government Association"],
 "The Care Quality Commission (CQC) registers, monitors and inspects health and adult social care services in England. Skills for Care supports workforce development but does not inspect."),
("A person you support asks you to do a task you have not been trained to do. What should you do?",
 "Explain and ask your manager", ["Do it carefully this once", "Ask the person to show you how", "Ask a visitor to do it instead"],
 "You must work within your competence. Explain kindly that you cannot do it yet and report to your manager so it is done safely and you can be trained."),
("Which code describes the expected behaviour of healthcare support workers and adult social care workers in England?",
 "The Code of Conduct for support workers", ["The NMC Code for nurses and midwives", "The Social Care Workforce Code", "The Caldicott principles of practice"],
 "The Code of Conduct for Healthcare Support Workers and Adult Social Care Workers in England sets out the standards of behaviour expected. The NMC Code applies to registered nurses and midwives."),
("What is an advocate's main role for a person who uses care services?",
 "To help them have their say", ["To make decisions for them", "To manage their money for them", "To give their personal care"],
 "An advocate supports a person to express their views and wishes and to stand up for their rights. They do not take over decisions the person can make."),
("Why is it important to work in partnership with a person's family and friends?",
 "They know the person well", ["They decide the care plan", "They replace care staff", "They check your rota"],
 "Family and friends often know the person's history, preferences and routines. Working with them, with the person's agreement, gives better person-centred care."),
("You disagree with how a senior colleague does a task. What is the best first step?",
 "Raise it calmly through proper channels", ["Copy them so the team stays consistent", "Tell the people you support about it", "Post about it on social media"],
 "Concerns about practice should be raised through agreed channels, calmly and professionally. Complaining to people who use the service or online breaks confidentiality and trust."),
("Your manager asks to meet you every few weeks to discuss your work and wellbeing. What is this meeting called?",
 "Supervision", ["A disciplinary", "An induction", "A handover"],
 "Supervision is a regular meeting with your manager to review your practice, support you and agree development. An appraisal is usually a yearly review of performance."),
("What is a personal development plan?",
 "Your learning goals and how to meet them", ["A list of your shifts for the next month", "A summary of a person's care needs", "A plan for a fire evacuation of the home"],
 "A personal development plan records your learning goals, how you will achieve them, the support you need and when you will review them."),
("What does reflective practice mean?",
 "Thinking back on your work to improve", ["Copying how others do each care task", "Writing up every task you finish", "Repeating the same training every year"],
 "Reflective practice means looking back at what happened, what went well, what could be better and what you will do differently. It helps you keep improving."),
("A goal in your development plan says: \"Complete moving and handling training by 30 June.\" Which SMART element does the date give it?",
 "Time-bound", ["Specific", "Achievable", "Relevant"],
 "SMART goals are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound. The deadline makes the goal time-bound."),
("Who can give you useful feedback on your work?",
 "People you support and colleagues", ["Only your line manager or deputy", "Only CQC inspectors on a visit", "Only your trainer on a course"],
 "Feedback can come from the people you support, their families, colleagues and managers. Each sees your work from a different angle."),
("Which digital skill does a care worker most often need at work?",
 "Recording care on a care app", ["Building a website for the service", "Repairing the office computers", "Writing software for care plans"],
 "Many services now keep care records electronically, so workers need to use approved digital tools safely and securely as part of their role."),
("What should you do if you receive feedback you find hard to hear?",
 "Listen and think about how to use it", ["Argue the point until it is withdrawn", "Ignore it if you do not agree with it", "Ask to be moved to a different manager"],
 "Feedback is a chance to learn. Listen, ask questions to understand it and agree with your manager how you will act on it."),
("Your values do not match the care choice a person makes. What should guide your actions?",
 "The person's wishes and care plan", ["Your own beliefs about what is best", "What most people would choose", "What is quickest for the team"],
 "Person-centred practice means respecting the person's choices, as set out in their care plan, even when you would choose differently. Raise any safety worries with your manager."),
("What is continuing professional development (CPD)?",
 "Learning throughout your career", ["Training given only in your first week", "A test you sit once each year", "A pay rise given after each course"],
 "CPD is the learning you keep doing throughout your working life so your knowledge and skills stay up to date."),
("When should you ask for help with your own learning needs?",
 "As soon as you notice a gap", ["Only at your yearly appraisal", "Only after a mistake", "Only when told to by CQC"],
 "Raise learning needs early, in supervision or before, so you are supported before a gap affects the people you care for."),
("Why must you know the limits of your role?",
 "To keep people and yourself safe", ["To avoid taking on any extra tasks", "To finish every shift on time", "To be promoted sooner than others"],
 "Staying within your role and competence protects people from harm and protects you. Tasks outside it should be referred to the right person."),
("A person you support sends you a friend request on social media. What should you do?",
 "Politely decline and stay professional", ["Accept it to build trust", "Accept it but never post anything", "Ignore it and tell nobody"],
 "Your relationship with the people you support is a working one with clear boundaries. Follow your employer's social media policy, decline kindly and tell your manager if it happens again."),
]
